# PetitePass rules

This is a **password manager**. Hold every change to a higher standard than an
ordinary app. The canonical guidance is [AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md) and the
threat model / invariants in [docs/DESIGN.md](../../docs/DESIGN.md).

## Hard constraints (do not regress)

- **Authentication is decryption.** A session is valid only if the vault
  decrypts *and* the `password` table is present. Never reintroduce a sidecar
  verifier or a boolean "is authenticated" flag.
- **Safe key path; no hand-built PRAGMA.** Key material reaches SQLCipher only
  through a reviewed, injection-safe keying path — today peewee's escaping
  `passphrase=` / `rekey()` API. Never
  `execute_sql(f"PRAGMA key = '{master}'")`.
- **Reject empty/NUL master** before creating or replacing any file.
- **Atomic vault-file replacement.** Rekey / restore / legacy relocation
  (`paths._migrate`) work on a *verified copy* and commit at a single
  `os.replace`; `fsync` of the copy is fatal. *Before* the commit a failure
  leaves the previous vault openable under the current master. *After* the
  commit the vault is the new one (only `paths._migrate`, whose post-commit work
  is pure cleanup, never flips). In-database schema changes don't replace the
  file; they use a transaction (DESIGN §12).
- **Distinct post-commit errors.** A reopen failure *after* `os.replace` is
  `VaultRotatedError` / `VaultRestoredError`, never an auth error. The change
  committed; the old master no longer opens the vault. Don't swallow it or call
  it "unchanged".
- **The `except Exception` in `Vault.create` / `rekey` / `restore_from` is
  deliberate** — it stops a NUL/`ValueError` from leaving a half-written file;
  do not narrow it. GUI slots catch `VaultError` only.
- **GUI never touches the ORM.** All credential access goes through `VAULT.*`;
  the service translates peewee `DatabaseError` into `VaultError`; GUI slots
  catch `VaultError`.
- **Never load password ciphertext to render the list** (`list_credentials`
  selects non-password columns).

These are the security core; the full set of 12 invariants is in DESIGN §16.
Weigh any new feature against the non-goals (DESIGN §2) and the
feature-admission checklist (DESIGN §4) — declining a feature is a normal outcome.

## Workflow

- Verify against **real SQLCipher**; do not mock the database in security tests.
- Test success paths for real too, not only mocked failures; platform-sensitive
  durability code must run on the OS it targets.
- Run `make check` (lint, typecheck, tests, headless GUI smoke test) before
  proposing a change.
- Keep the runtime dependency set minimal; `pip-audit` gates CI.
- Keep docstrings/README truthful to the code.
